<section class="rcx-mush" aria-label="The mushrooms"><div class="rcx-m m-lions-mane"><div class="rcx-symt"><img class="rcx-sym" src="{{ 'rcx-sym-lions-mane.svg' | asset_url }}" alt=""></div><h3>LION'S MANE</h3><p class="lat">Hericium erinaceus</p><p class="pp"><b>PART</b> FRUITING BODY<br><b>PROCESS</b> HOT WATER + ALCOHOL</p><p class="kick2">ABOUT LION'S MANE</p><p class="ab">Lion's mane, <span class='rcx-ob'>a nootropic,</span> was first named Hydnum erinaceus in 1781. In 1797 it was moved into its own genus, Hericium, and it carries that name today. Erinaceus is Latin for hedgehog, and the common name comes from the long white spines that hang like a lion's mane.</p><div class="lab"><span>Hericium erinaceus fruiting body</span><span>80:20 water alcohol extract</span><span>Beta&#8209;glucan / Megazyme K&#8209;YBGL</span><span>Heavy metals / ICP&#8209;MS ISO 17294&#8209;2</span><span>539 pesticides / EN 15662, EN 12393</span></div></div><div class="rcx-m m-cordyceps"><div class="rcx-symt"><img class="rcx-sym" src="{{ 'rcx-sym-cordyceps.svg' | asset_url }}" alt=""></div><h3>CORDYCEPS</h3><p class="lat">Cordyceps militaris</p><p class="pp"><b>PART</b> FRUITING BODY<br><b>PROCESS</b> HOT WATER</p><p class="kick2">ABOUT CORDYCEPS</p><p class="ab">Cordyceps, <span class='rcx-ob'>an adaptogen,</span> was first described by Linnaeus in 1753 as Clavaria militaris. In 1818 Elias Fries moved it into the genus Cordyceps, where it became the type species, the one that defines the genus.</p><div class="lab"><span>Cordyceps militaris fruiting body</span><span>Hot water extract</span><span>Beta&#8209;glucan / Megazyme K&#8209;YBGL</span><span>Cordycepin, adenosine / HPLC</span><span>Heavy metals / ICP&#8209;MS ISO 17294&#8209;2</span><span>539 pesticides / EN 15662, EN 12393</span></div></div><div class="rcx-m m-turkey-tail"><div class="rcx-symt"><img class="rcx-sym" src="{{ 'rcx-sym-turkey-tail.svg' | asset_url }}" alt=""></div><h3>TURKEY TAIL</h3><p class="lat">Trametes versicolor</p><p class="pp"><b>PART</b> MYCELIUM<br><b>PROCESS</b> HOT WATER</p><p class="kick2">ABOUT TURKEY TAIL</p><p class="ab">Turkey tail, <span class='rcx-ob'>a prebiotic,</span> was first named Boletus versicolor in 1753 by Linnaeus. It moved to Polyporus in 1821, Coriolus in 1886 and Trametes in 1920, its name today. Versicolor is Latin for many colors, and the common name comes from the fan of colored bands that looks like a wild turkey's tail.</p><div class="lab"><span>Trametes versicolor mycelium</span><span>Hot water extract</span><span>Beta&#8209;glucan / Megazyme K&#8209;YBGL</span><span>Heavy metals / ICP&#8209;MS ISO 17294&#8209;2</span><span>539 pesticides / EN 15662, EN 12393</span></div></div></section>

<section class="rcx-box">
<h2 data-sweep>WHY ONE INGREDIENT?</h2>
<p>Look at the back of most mushroom products. Eight ingredients. Twelve. Twenty. A blend, a complex, a stack. It reads like abundance. It works like a smoke screen. When everything is in there, nobody can tell you how much of any one thing you're getting, and nobody has to.</p>
<p>Ours has one ingredient. The mushroom. <span class="rcx-ob">It's certified organic,</span> measured into one clean 2 gram packet to take anywhere you go.</p>
<p>Nothing to carry it, nothing to fill it out, nothing to make the label longer. Which means every number stands on its own, and to prove it, we will publish the independent lab report we call the receipts, just one scan away. Build your own honest blend and bring the best of the forest to your coffee, tea or&nbsp;smoothie.</p>
<p class="rcx-prove" data-sweep>THEY PROMISE. WE PROVE.</p>
</section>
